Simple Web Stack - www.foobar.com
==================================

Infrastructure overview
------------------------

A user types www.foobar.com in their browser. Here's what happens:

1. The browser asks DNS "what IP is www.foobar.com?" — DNS answers 8.8.8.8
2. The browser sends an HTTP request to 8.8.8.8
3. Nginx receives the request on the server
4. If it's a static file (image, CSS...), Nginx serves it directly
5. If it's dynamic, Nginx passes it to the application server
6. The app server runs the code, talks to MySQL if needed, builds the response
7. The response travels back to the user's browser

Everything runs on a single server at IP 8.8.8.8.

```mermaid
graph TD
    User["User Browser"]
    DNS["DNS\nA record: www.foobar.com → 8.8.8.8"]

    User -->|"1. DNS query"| DNS
    DNS -->|"2. returns 8.8.8.8"| User
    User -->|"3. HTTP/HTTPS request via TCP/IP"| Nginx

    subgraph Server["Server — IP: 8.8.8.8"]
        Nginx["Nginx\nWeb Server"]
        App["Application Server"]
        Files["Application Files\ncodebase"]
        MySQL["MySQL\nDatabase"]

        Nginx -->|"dynamic request"| App
        App --- Files
        App <-->|"SQL read/write"| MySQL
    end

    Nginx -->|"4. HTTP response"| User
```


Q&A
----

What is a server?
  A computer (physical or virtual) that listens for requests from clients and
  sends back responses — in this case, web pages.

What is the role of the domain name?
  It's a human-friendly alias for the server's IP. Nobody wants to remember
  8.8.8.8, so foobar.com points there instead.

What type of DNS record is "www" in www.foobar.com?
  It's an A record — it maps the hostname www.foobar.com to the IPv4 address 8.8.8.8.

What is the role of the web server (Nginx)?
  It's the front door. It receives HTTP/HTTPS requests, serves static files itself,
  and forwards anything dynamic to the application server.

What is the role of the application server?
  It runs the actual code. It processes the request, applies business logic,
  talks to the database if needed, and returns a response to Nginx.

What is the role of the database (MySQL)?
  It stores all persistent data — users, content, etc. The app server queries it
  with SQL whenever it needs to read or write data.

How does the server communicate with the user?
  Over HTTP (port 80) or HTTPS (port 443), running on top of TCP/IP.


Issues with this infrastructure
---------------------------------

SPOF (Single Point of Failure)
  Everything is on one server. If it dies, the whole site goes down.
  There's no backup or failover.

Downtime during maintenance
  Restarting Nginx or deploying new code takes the site offline.
  With only one server, there's no way to do a rolling update.

Can't scale with traffic
  All components share the same machine's CPU and RAM.
  If traffic spikes, you can't just add more servers — the design doesn't allow it.


